WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.560
The Interdisciplinary Journal of Planetary
Ethics recently received three

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.320
climate research articles. They came from
a high-profile institute and

3
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.800
contained massive, highly complex
datasets.

4
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.800
The authors requested that all publication
fees be waived.

5
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.960
Their justification was that the research
was completely publicly funded,

6
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.520
and they argued the findings held urgent
global relevance.

7
00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.800
This graphic shows the economic friction
at play.

8
00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.520
Evaluating these specific papers requires
intense editorial labor and

9
00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.800
significant data handling infrastructure
on the journal's side,

10
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.280
which directly conflicts with the author's
request to waive the fee.

11
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.460
If the journal waives the fee and the name
of the public good,

12
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.380
who actually pays for the infrastructure
required to verify the science?
